Hello everyone,

As mentioned last week, after [~accountid:609bd1fadafdf0006806ed70] and I explored options to circumvent Tenerife's fixed price regulation without finding any completely satisfactory solution, PP and legal decided to jointly instruct an external lawyer to advise us on this matter.

Below you will find the external counsel’s advice. Unfortunately, it seems that the only solution lies with Public Policy to try to convince a modification of the current regulation:

{color:#008da6}Please find below our analysis regarding the possibility of offering fixed prices in advance for taxi drivers in Tenerife (Canary Islands) joining the Bolt platform.{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}+*A. Applicable regulatory framework*+{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}                    i.        Law 13/2007, of 17 May, on the Regulation of Road Transport in the Canary Islands.{color}

{color:#008da6}                   ii.        Decree 72/2012 of 2 August CA Canary Islands (Regulation of development of Law 13/2007 of 17 May, Regulation of Road Transport).{color}

{color:#008da6}                 iii.        Decree 74/2012 of 2 August CA Canary Islands (regulation of the Taxi Service).{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}Pursuant to these regulations, one of the main regulatory principles governing taxi activities in Canarias is that fares to be collected by taxi drivers from users and set by the corresponding local councils (article 80 et seq. of Law 13/2007) are compulsory. However, applicable regulations (article 18 of Decree 74/2012) establish 2 exceptions to the general principle on fares that could allow to agree on a fixed rate for the ride and exempt taxi drivers from using a taximeter: (i) ride for a specific route and a duration of more than 3 hours; or (ii) in those cases in which transport is provided on demand, in accordance with the provisions of article 68 of Law 13/2007.{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}+*B. Scenario (i): ride of more than 3h duration*+{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}The purpose of the regulation is to cover rides with a single user and a pre-determined duration and route. In these cases, it is compulsory to the taxi driver to carry on in the vehicle the relevant documents containing the number of users per ride, time and place of start and end of the service, price agreed and signature and ID number of the taxi driver and the user.{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}The rationale behind this exception is that only specific long duration rides can be exempted from the application of the fares. The evidence of the relevant document to be carried out by taxi drivers (through the working of the T&C) in our opinion would be insufficient to prove that this is a single ride with a predetermined duration of more than 3 hours. In fact, any such attempt to try to justify a single trip of more than 3 hours when in practice are regular taxi rides below 3 hours, may be seen by authorities as a fraud of law.{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}Thus, it seems difficult to fit Bolt's proposal in this context by justifying through a private contract that it is a single trip of more than 3 hours duration with a single user, even if Bolt tries to justify that, in fact, the three-hour threshold is met considering the duration of several trips made by the same passenger as an open-ended transportation service. Furthermore, another point that may make challenging that option would be the fixed price to be shown in the relevant documents that the taxi driver should have, as in most cases such fares/prices would be very low or, alternatively, would not reflect the relevant trip.{color}



{color:#008da6}Finally, another option would be for Bolt to be the one directly contracting the rides with the taxi drivers, as the company receiving such transportation services. However, we anticipate, at least, three issues around such possibility: (i) Bolt would be putting at risk its position as a mere intermediary; (ii) the applicable legislation seems to be thinking about this as an exception addressed to individuals and not companies; (iii) our understanding is that Scenario (ii) below is probably the one intended to be available for companies to address the request of such services.{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}+*C. Scenario (ii): transport on demand for occasional passenger and freight services*+{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}The slightly grey wording of Law 13/2007 on the concept of transport on demand ({color}{color:#008da6}_transporte a la demanda_{color}{color:#008da6}) and its regime leaves open the possibility for certain subjects, in specific situations, to offer rides with a price fixed in advance.{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}Article 67 of Law 13/2007 defines transport on demand as regular transport services offered to specific categories of passengers at their request, directly or through entities representing them, or through the companies in which they work, excluding other passengers, based on pre-agreed and determined frequencies, routes, stops and even temporary periods of service. In this case, the price can be agreed by means of a prior contract (article 68 of Law 13/2007), subject to special authorisation by the competent authority. However, the aforementioned article 68 establishes the obligation to check that the agreed transport does not overlap with the services provided by the public service operator in the area, if there is one (i.e., public service buses).{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}Article 67.2 of Law 13/2007 introduces an exception to this transport on demand, allowing its regime to be extended to occasional passenger and freight services ({color}{color:#008da6}_servicios discrecionales de pasajeros y de mercancías_{color}{color:#008da6}), as long as the measures applicable to the transport on demand regime set out in article 70 of Decree 72/2012 are adopted. Among all of them, requirement d) appears to be the most important impediment for taxis on Bolt´s platform to provide services qualifying as transport on demand, since it requires taxi drivers to file a responsible declaration, prior to the authorisation granted by the relevant public authorities, stating the route, {color}{color:#008da6}+frequency+{color}{color:#008da6}, and schedule. In addition, it should be noted that the term of the authorisation will be the same as that established in the contract or pre-contract, so that the aforementioned declaration, which will be checked by the competent public authority prior to the resolution, should be sent each time a new trip is intended -if the destinations are different and there are no predetermined routes- to be carried out. Please note that taxi drivers are exempted from this authorisation to provide transport on demand services, but not from submitting the above-mentioned prior responsible declaration to operate on that basis.{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}Another element to take into account is that transport on demand is related, in our opinion, only to cases similar to:{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

* {color:#008da6}shuttles of workers to their workplace or as an alternative for certain services; or{color}
* {color:#008da6}those of very small municipalities where no public transportation -e.g., bus lines- are available for consumers and authorities intend to allow such services as a regular cost-friendly alternative for individuals -e.g., individuals that do not have a private vehicle and that need a regular ride once per week to visit a medical centre-.{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}In view of the above, we understand that the mere amendment of the T&Cs to fall within such exemption would not be sufficient to meet the requirements of the law and may certainly increase the risk profile both for the taxi driver and Bolt. After all, the authorisation to establish the price for providers of occasional passenger services responds, as in scenario (i), to a context where the route is known, {color}{color:#008da6}+regular and on a specific schedule+{color}{color:#008da6}, and has been agreed in advance by a single user or a company, and as such, the authorities require to know all this information (through the prior responsible declaration) before granting the authorisation. Another point is that the user benefitting from such services could be a tourist or a foreigner that do not reside in the Canary Islands and, thus, might not fall within the type of beneficiaries of such on demand services that the legislation intends to cover under the exemptions.{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}+*D. Potential consequences*+{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}Considering both scenarios that would allow to set a fixed taxi fare and that any eventual amendments to the T&Cs with users and taxi drivers would be deemed to circumvent the compulsory application of the relevant taxi fares for rides that, in practice, are below 3 hours and that do not meet the requirements for transport on demand, the following consequences should be noted:{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}                    i.        Article 6.4 of the Spanish Civil Code sets forth that acts carried out pursuant to the text of a legal provision (as it would be the case through article 18 of Decree 74/2012 or article 70 of Decree 72/2012), which pursue a result forbidden by the legal system or contrary thereto (to circumvent the application of compulsory fares for single rides with a duration below 3 hours or for rides with a specific frequency) shall be deemed to be in fraud of the law and shall not prevent due application of the provision which they purported to elude.{color}

{color:#008da6}                   ii.        Any provision on the relevant T&Cs, either between Bolt and the taxi driver or with the users, would in our opinion fit article 6 of the Spanish Civil Code and deemed to be carried out in fraud of law.{color}

{color:#008da6}                 iii.        The immediate consequence would be that the specific wording included in the T&Cs to circumvent the application of the compulsory taxi fares would be deemed null and void and, thus, would have no effect.{color}

{color:#008da6}                  iv.        We cannot ignore the fact that, in the event of non-compliance with the requirements described above, administrative sanctions could be imposed:{color}

{color:#008da6}                                  a.        Bolt could be considered responsible as the entity arranging the relevant services between taxi drivers and passengers (article 102 of Law 13/2007) and be subject to economic fines and even disqualifications to carry out relevant transport activities; and{color}

{color:#008da6}                                  b.        taxi drivers could be subject not only to potential economic fines, but also to the revocation of their licences to provide the taxi service, declared of public interest by the regulations (article 102 et seq. of Law 13/2007).{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}+*E. Conclusion and alternatives*+{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}To summarise, from our analysis we understand that the possibility of offering fixed taxi prices in advance in Canarias by taxi drivers who join Bolt's platform entails several risks from a legal point of view, unless they truly entail one single trip between a single user and a single taxi driver and with a duration of more than 3 hours or a specific frequency. Such risks could not be significantly mitigated by amending the T&Cs and may imply the imposition of potential fines and penalties applicable to both taxi drivers and Bolt for non-compliance with the established requirements.{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}In our view, the risk of operating with a fixed price just by amending the T&Cs would be relatively high and would entail, at the very least, a significant reputational risk for Bolt, depending on follow-up actions that may be initiated by taxi associations, authorities or even competitors already operating there.{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}Please find below a couple of alternatives to be evaluated by Bolt:{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

* {color:#008da6}_Amendments to the existing pieces of legislation_{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}An option to be explored to include fares that allow pre-agreed prices is the amendment of the relevant regulations, at the proposal of the different authorities in charge of setting said prices.{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}Thus, Article 17 of Decree 74/2012 authorises the regional authority responsible for road transport to modify the amounts and parameters of the fares approved by the Government of the Canary Islands (i.e., interurban fares) and to establish flat fares at the request of the municipal corporation. This procedure can be initiated ex officio by said municipal corporation or at the request of the most representative associations of the taxi transport sector in that municipality (giving a hearing to all of them), when there is a variation in costs that significantly alters the economic balance.{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}With regard to urban fares, which are the responsibility of the local council, article 9 of Decree 64/2000, of 25 April, which regulates the procedure for the introduction and modification of prices of goods and services authorised and communicated at regional level, also authorises price modifications requested when they are justified (by means of the appropriate economic studies and analyses) in relation to variations in production or commercialisation costs or, where appropriate, variations in the features of the service in question.{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}In view of the above, we understand that if the most representative associations of the taxi transport sector request in a reasoned manner the need to establish fixed fares due to the variations in the service caused by the entry of VTC (also based on economic and free-market terms), it could be possible to try to initiate a fare modification procedure and try to discuss the approach of the current regulation with the authorities.{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

* {color:#008da6}_Operation based on Scenario (ii)_{color}

{color:#008da6} {color}

{color:#008da6}Another alternative, for the time being, would be to establish a contractual scheme by means of which Bolt, acting on behalf of the users, decide to execute an agreement with the relevant taxi based on the scenario (ii). In such a case, it would be necessary to predefine some routes/schedules and the relevant fare applicable by the taxi to be submitted together with the responsible declaration. If taxis intend to cooperate and such scheme works for the relevant authorities -subject to the limitations that no other public transport alternative is identified by authorities for such route-, Bolt may initially launch in Canarias under that framework until relevant amendments are adopted in the applicable legislation. Although not ideal from a commercial standpoint, this may work as an interim solution if authorities do not raise any concern, as they would be aware of such activity and the framework used by taxis for doing so.{color}



